# Environment Variables

Never read `import.meta.env` or `process.env` directly in app code. All access
goes through a single validated module so a missing/malformed var fails at boot,
not at runtime.

## 1. `src/lib/env.ts`
- Define one `zod` schema for every Vite client var the app uses (`VITE_*`).
- Parse `import.meta.env` once at module load; `throw` with a readable message
  listing what's missing.
- Export a typed `env` object.

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const schema = z.object({
  VITE_API_URL: z.string().url(),
  VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY: z.string().min(1),
  VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID: z.string().min(1),
  VITE_FIREBASE_VAPID_KEY: z.string().min(1),
  // …one line per var, all of them
});

const parsed = schema.safeParse(import.meta.env);
if (!parsed.success) {
  throw new Error(
    `Invalid environment variables:\n${parsed.error.issues
      .map((i) => `  - ${i.path.join('.')}: ${i.message}`)
      .join('\n')}`,
  );
}

export const env = parsed.data;
```

## 2. Rules
- Import `env` from `@/lib/env`; never `import.meta.env.X` or `process.env.X`
  outside this file.
- Only `VITE_`-prefixed vars are exposed to the client bundle. Anything secret
  (private API keys, tokens) must stay on the backend — do not put it in Vite
  client env.
- Add every new var to the schema **and** to `.env.example` (committed, with
  placeholder values) in the same change.
- No fallback literals for required vars (`import.meta.env.X ?? 'https://…'`) —
  if it's required, let validation fail.
- Firebase config and `VITE_API_URL` (@api-client.md) read from `env`.
